@meta
id: 10-10
title: Inside the argument list
chapter: 10
type: lesson
skills: obj-paren
par: 80
minutes: 6
vscode: click after the open paren, shift-End, fix the end by hand
open: order.provider.ts

@brief
`i(` is everything between a matching pair of round brackets. In a
TypeScript file that means the argument list of a call or the parameter
list of a function, which between them are most of the brackets you will
ever edit.

`ci(` is the edit that follows: wipe the arguments, type the new ones,
leave the brackets alone. It is the same shape of move as `ci"` and it is
the second one worth having in your fingers.

One difference from the quote objects, and it matters: the cursor has to
be inside the brackets, or on one of them. Vim will not go looking down
the line for the next pair the way it does for a string. `f(` first.

The payment service has changed under you. `refund` now needs the amount
as well as the order id, and `cancel` has to take a reason so the refund
call can be audited. Update both signatures:

  - `cancel(id: string, reason: string)`
  - `refund(order.id, order.total)`

The body of the update call is already correct.

@keys
| `i(` | everything between the brackets |
| `ci(` | change the argument list |
| `i)` `ib` | the same object, two more names for it |
| `f(` | get inside the brackets first |

@hints
The argument list is a thing with two edges, like a string. There is an
object for it.
--
`ci(` replaces everything between the brackets and leaves the brackets.
`i)` and `ib` are the same object if you prefer the other key.
--
Unlike `ci"`, this one will not search forward for you. `f(` puts the
cursor on the bracket, which counts as inside, and then `ci(` works.

@solution
    j f( ci( id: string, reason: string <Esc>
    3j 0 f( ci( order.id, order.total <Esc>
    :wq<Enter>

Sixty-seven keystrokes, fifty of them the new argument lists.
Everything else is navigation.

`ci(` is worth more than it first looks because of what it protects you
from. Changing an argument list by hand means finding the closing bracket,
and closing brackets are the single most common thing to get wrong when
selecting — one too few and you leave a fragment, one too many and you
have broken the call and will not see it until the compiler complains.
The object cannot make that mistake: it is defined by the matching pair.

`b` in `ib` and `ab` stands for bracket. `i(`, `i)` and `ib` all mean the
same thing, so use whichever your hand reaches first. Some people prefer
`i)` because you press the same shift as for the closing bracket you are
looking at; some prefer `ib` because it needs no shift at all.

@start order.provider.ts
export class OrderProvider {
  public async cancel(id: string) {
    const order = await this.findOne(id);

    await this.paymentService.refund(order.id);

    return this.orderRepository.update({
      where: { id },
      values: { status: 'CANCELLED' },
    });
  }
}

@expected order.provider.ts
export class OrderProvider {
  public async cancel(id: string, reason: string) {
    const order = await this.findOne(id);

    await this.paymentService.refund(order.id, order.total);

    return this.orderRepository.update({
      where: { id },
      values: { status: 'CANCELLED' },
    });
  }
}
